V1="VARIANT 1: THE BANDED DECK, daytime storm. Long wind-sheared diagonal bands of layered cloud, like a shear zone: smooth stretched cloud sheets sliding over each other, rolled shelf edges and a few cyclonic curl scars, relatively smooth with fine wispy striations and only modest billowing. Mid steel-blue and teal-grey, pale cool highlights on the sheared lips, thin lightning glowing inside the seams between bands."
V2="VARIANT 2: THE THUNDERHEAD FIELD, daytime storm. Many towering cumulonimbus tops and overshooting domes seen from above, in clusters of very different sizes (huge dome, mid towers, small buds), with long soft cast shadows and anvil spreads smoothing off into sheared wisps; canyons between the towers hold glowing lightning. Strong top lighting from one side, cool steel-white highlights."
V3="VARIANT 3: THE NIGHT SIDE. Near-black blue graphite storm cloud seen from above at night: huge slow billows and canyons, lit only from INSIDE by sheet and fork lightning: soft blue-white and pale violet-white blooms of light glowing up through gaps and under cloud lips, branching lightning veins buried in the canyons, cloud edges rimmed in cold blue-white. Overall dark (average brightness about 0.15 to 0.25) but every cloud form still readable."
V4="VARIANT 4: THE GUST FRONT. A storm outflow from above: huge nested scalloped SHELF ARCS, successive curved roll-cloud fronts sweeping across the frame in broad overlapping crescents, each arc a long ridge of tightly rolled cloud with a cool steel-white lit crest edge and a deep blue-teal shadow trough behind it, calm dark slate cloud between arcs, drifting rain curtains veiling parts of the troughs, thin lightning threads in the troughs. Mostly diagonal and curved flow, NOT vertical towers."
